// A buffer's byte index can be a NAME as well as a number: a #define or a
// #constant, anything that is known when the program is built. It used to take
// an integer literal only, so `Frame[BYTEPOS]` was a syntax error -- the kind of
// name a CAN map (frame, bytepos, bitmask) wants to give it.
//
// A #param is refused, with its own error: a byte view's position is fixed at
// compile time and a param can change after the build.

#define LO  0
#define HI  1
#constant MID = 1

#buffer Frame:16
#variable B0 = 0
#variable B1 = 0
#variable Rng = 0
#variable M = 0

Frame[LO] = 52 ? 1           // write through a #define
Frame[HI] = 18 ? 1
B0 = Frame[LO] ? 1           // read through a #define
B1 = Frame[HI] ? 1
Rng = Frame[LO..HI] ? 1      // a range, both ends named
M = Frame[MID] ? 1           // and a #constant
